# Roadmap

The phase registry for this project. Every phase this project has is
declared here, and every task outside `tasks/triage/` is listed under
exactly one of them.

Two facts are kept in step mechanically, so neither can drift:

- a task's `phase:` must equal a phase id declared here, and
- every task carrying a phase must appear under that phase in this file.
  (`triage/` tasks have no phase yet; a task closed straight out of triage
  keeps none, so neither appears here.)

Tasks in `tasks/triage/` are deliberately phase-less and do **not** appear
here. Putting one here, or giving one a `phase:`, is an error -- graduate it
first.

---

## The heading format, exactly

Get this wrong and nothing matches anything, so here it is spelled out. Each
phase is one `##` heading followed by a scope paragraph and a list of tasks:

```
  ## Phase DISC — Discovery

  Scope. Two to four sentences: what is in, what is out, and what finishing
  this phase looks like. This paragraph is the contract for the phase.

  - TASK-001 — Draft the first-contact questionnaire
  - TASK-002 — Agree the retention schedule
```

> **The two leading spaces in that block are not part of the format.** They
> are there so the example is not read as a real phase. Write your real
> headings and task lines flush against the left margin, with no indent.

The pieces:

- **`## Phase `** -- literally that, at the start of the line.
- **`<id>`** -- one word, no spaces: `1`, `2.1`, `INTAKE`, `DISC`. This is
  the string that goes in a task's `phase:` field, and it is matched
  **case-sensitively**. Two headings that yield the same id is an error.
- **A separator, then the name.** An em dash (as above), an en dash, a single
  plain hyphen, or a colon straight after the id. A doubled `--` is **not** a
  separator and will not be read as one. The name must not be empty.
- **The scope paragraph** -- at least one real line before the next heading.
  A phase with no scope is a label, not a plan, and the validator says so.
- **The task lines** -- the id, a separator, the title; one per task, each
  starting with `-` or `*`, exactly as shown above. Their order is your
  suggested order of work, and nothing ever re-sorts them.

Do not put a task id under two phases, and do not leave a line here naming a
task that no longer exists. Both are errors, and the second one is never
fixed automatically -- a line naming a missing file is either a lost task or
a promise nobody kept, and only you know which.

